//   try to test some more instructions
//   try to check for missing instructions
//   check worst case use of scratchregs... (mem op mem -> mem with large int offsets)

// compile guard
' DGARM64CROSSASM.DGLU ENDOFWORDLIST = 0= ?END-EVALUATE
0 CONSTANT DGARM64CROSSASM.DGLU


// Arm calling convention:
//
//   Ints:
//     passed in X0 X1 X2 X3 X4 X5 X6 X7 
//     passed out X0 X1
//     must be preserved X19 - X28
//     linker special use X16 X17 ( linkers may corrupt these between a call and first instruction of a subroutine )
//     indirect result register X9, points to caller allocated memory used to return structures 
//     frame pointer X29 
//     link register X30 ( used to hold the return address for function calls )
//
//   Floats:
//     passed in D0 D1 D2 D3 D4 D5 D6 D7
//     passed out D0 D1 D2 D3 D4 D5 D6 D7


// Mac calling convention:
//
//    Ints:
//      X18 platforms reserve register, must not be used.
//      X29 frame pointer register, must always point to a valid frame record which is the previous frame pointer
//
// Arm64 notes:
//   Specifying register X31 can refer to the current return stack pointer, a source integer value 0, or no destination depending on the instruction.
//     In general, register X31 refers to the current return stack pointer when the instruction does not set the condition code flags.
//   Return stack pointer must be 16 byte aligned when memory is accessed using the return stack pointer. Not sure if interrupts can happen at any time,
//     if so... return stack pointer must always be 16 byte aligned... unless you disable interrupts or know exactly when they will happen...

// Mac Arm64 M4 features supported:
//   Features supported:
//     FEAT_FLAGm


PCURRENTCOMPILEBUFFER @ CONSTANT dgarm64crossasmbufid

HEX
55 ( for stack check at end )

: ?MARKER ( u1 u1 -- u ) ( -e- |errormessage )
    OVER <> IF ERROR" marker missing" THEN
;

